---
// Lesson 6.4a — Boots: Double Jump
// A pickup that changes capability AGAIN — and a jump counter.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>6.4a — Boots: Double Jump</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The axe granted an attack. The potion capped a number. The boots
    grant <em>movement</em> — a second jump mid-air — and that one
    item restructures your whole level design, because every gap,
    wall, and secret is now jumpable twice. Capability items rewrite
    the world.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>You already know the shape (<a href="5.1a.html">5.1a</a>):
    pickup → bool → capability. Predict the three pieces before
    writing them:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Piece</th><th>Your prediction</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Inventory field</td><td>private bool ___ = false</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pickup branch</td><td>CompareTag("___") → set true, Destroy</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jump logic</td><td>??? — and HERE is the new question: how do you count jumps in mid-air?</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>The trap: isGrounded (from <a href="6.2a.html">6.2a</a>) can
    answer "may I jump?" but not "is this my FIRST or SECOND jump?"
    Mid-air, grounded is false for both. You need a
    <strong>counter</strong> — jumps used — that resets when you land.
    Predict its type, its reset point, and the exact condition:</p>
    <pre><code>if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space) &amp;&amp; ??? )
{
    ???
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            A bool says one jump; a counter says any number of jumps;
            the AP exam says: choose the abstraction that matches the
            problem. If your game later adds WING BOOTS for a third
            jump, which of your lines changes — and which one
            sentence would you write for the Create PT to justify
            this data choice?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the counter.</strong> Add to PlayerController,
    in the starter's voice:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private int maxJumps = 1;   // boots raise this
private int jumpsUsed;

// reset the moment you touch ground (right where isGrounded is set):
if (isGrounded) jumpsUsed = 0;

// the jump condition becomes:
if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space) &amp;&amp; jumpsUsed &lt; maxJumps)
{
    rb.AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse);
    jumpsUsed++;
}</code></pre>
    <p>Run it: still one jump (1 used &lt; 1 max). The counter
    exists but changes nothing yet — deploy code in neutral, THEN
    flip the switch. That order keeps bugs boring.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the boots.</strong> Build the pickup exactly
    like the axe: sprite (a boot!), trigger collider, tag "Boots"
    (make the tag), and the branch:</p>
    <pre><code>if (other.CompareTag("Boots"))
{
    maxJumps = 2;
    // animator swap / particle flourish here if you have art
    Destroy(other.gameObject);
}</code></pre>
    <p>Press Play: one jump. Grab the boots. Two jumps. Now the
    LEVEL is different — every gap you tuned in
    <a href="6.1a.html">6.1a</a> just became trivial. Hold that
    thought for Step 4.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — merge with the mercies.</strong> Your jump
    condition from <a href="6.3a.html">6.3a</a> had coyote time and a
    buffer in it. Careful surgery: the buffer should NOT fire the
    second jump while you fall (buffer was for landing). Decide:
    second jump must be a DIRECT press in the air. Write the full
    condition: (grounded OR coyote) AND count-ok for the first;
    NOT grounded AND count-ok for the second. Test both jumps with
    late presses and early presses at a ledge edge. If it feels
    wrong, your two timer-paths are fighting — print them.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — redesign for capability.</strong> Place a
    coin where ONLY a double jump reaches. Now the level has a
    soft gate: no damage, no lock — just a visible "come back when
    you are more." That is a metroidvania in one sentence. The boots
    are not a power-up; they are a KEY made of physics.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The infinite stair</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Put maxJumps = 2 in the field's starting value
                (serialized!). The whole cave is now jumpable
                without boots — what "cost" did forgetting the
                default destroy?</li>
            <li>Reset jumpsUsed in Update instead of where
                isGrounded is set. Where does the reset happen when
                you are FALLING (isGrounded false) — and why is the
                second jump... not second?</li>
            <li>Grab the boots TWICE (two pickups on the map).
                maxJumps becomes 2 then 2. Should the second pair
                do NOTHING, or is there a design where stacking
                makes sense (3 jumps)? Who decides — code or
                designer?</li>
            <li>Second jump with FULL jumpForce feels like a
                stutter-step. Try 0.8 × jumpForce for the air jump.
                Which line needs to know WHICH jump number it
                is?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Capability design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Design a THIRD item (gloves → wall jump?) using the
                same pickup skeleton. What does the LEVEL gain
                access to — write two sentences like a designer
                selling it.</li>
            <li>Make the boots REQUIRED: seal the cave exit behind a
                high ledge. What does the player do when they hit the
                ledge before the boots — and is that frustration or
                curiosity?</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"6.4a: double jump + soft gate"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain counter vs bool for multi-jump and why the counter resets on landing</li>
            <li>Build the boots pickup and raise maxJumps</li>
            <li>Merge the counter with coyote/buffer without the buffer double-firing</li>
            <li>Design a soft gate (visible, unreachable, no damage)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/index.html">Unit 6: Jumping</a> — the double-jump design notes</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-05/index.html">Unit 5: The Axe &amp; The Cave</a> — the pickup family, compared</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="6.5a.html">6.5a — Platforming Studio: The Gauntlet</a></p>
</div>`;
